\chapter{Tutorial: Getting started}
\label{tutorial-getting-started}

[[getting-started]] is the first tutorial a new user of [[canvaslms]] should
run.
Its object of learning is the shape that every later command shares: a verb
names what to list, a positional regex or an option narrows the result, and
the output is tab-separated text meant to be piped onward.
A learner who finishes it has reached Canvas from the terminal, has seen
the difference between selecting rows and selecting columns, and has fed
the output of one command into another program.

The tutorial keeps the [[courses]] command invariant for four steps and
varies one aspect at a time around it, then repeats the pattern on the
[[users]] command so the pattern is seen to hold for more than one noun.
The critical aspects are these:
\begin{description}
\item[Credentials] A command reaches Canvas only after a login, and the
  login is created once and reused by every later command.
\item[Rows versus columns] The positional regex chooses \emph{which}
  courses are listed; an option such as [[-i]] chooses \emph{what is
  printed} about each of them.
\item[Terminal versus pipe] The output is tab-separated text, which is
  why it can be cut, counted and filtered by ordinary tools.
\item[Selecting a course] Every command that works inside a course takes
  [[-c]] with a regex, so [[-c]] is the one option worth memorising.
\end{description}

The arc is:
\begin{enumerate}
\item read the help (step~1) so the verb-and-options shape is seen before
  it is used;
\item reach Canvas (step~2), logging in only if that is needed;
\item contrast no filter with a filter (step~3), then rows with columns
  (step~4), then terminal with pipe (step~5);
\item generalise to [[users]] with the [[-c]] option (steps 6--7);
\item name the invariant in a question (step~8).
\end{enumerate}

\section{How the steps are validated}

The learner's Canvas is not ours, so the tutorial cannot know which
courses or which students the commands will print.
Every shell step therefore validates the \emph{typed command} rather
than the output, with a regex anchored on the command name:
[[(?:^|\s)canvaslms\s+courses\b]] accepts [[canvaslms courses]] as well as
[[poetry run canvaslms courses]], but not a stray mention of the word.
Output is matched only where it is deterministic, namely the
description line of the help text in step~1.
The nested tutorial shell reports no window size, so [[argparse]] wraps
its help at 80 columns everywhere, which is what makes even that match
safe.

None of the steps uses [[pre_command]], [[check_command]] or
[[post_command]], so the tutorial runs unchanged under
[[--no-allow-shell]].

\section{Tutorial source}

The whole tutorial is one tangled root chunk; each step is a named chunk
so the prose between them can explain that step's role.

<<[[getting-started.md]]>>=
---
id: getting-started
title: Getting started with canvaslms
summary: Log in, list your courses and users, and pipe the output onward.
---

<<read the help step>>

<<reach canvas step>>

<<filter courses step>>

<<add the id column step>>

<<pipe the output step>>

<<list users step>>

<<count users step>>

<<name the course option step>>
@

\section{Step 1: Ask canvaslms what it can do}

We start with [[-h]] because it is the only command that works before
anything else exists: no login, no course, no network.
It also shows the learner the whole verb list at once, so the later
steps pick verbs from a list the learner has already seen rather than
pulling them out of the air.
The help text is the one output we can validate: its description line,
[[Scriptable Canvas LMS]], is the same on every machine.

<<read the help step>>=
# Ask canvaslms what it can do

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)canvaslms\s+(?:-h|--help)\b
  - Scriptable Canvas LMS
hint: "Every canvaslms command answers to the same help flag. Ask the top-level command."
```

Run `canvaslms -h`.

The first line of the help is the shape of every command you will type
in this tutorial: `canvaslms`, a verb such as `courses` or `users`, and
then that verb's own options. The list under **commands** is the
complete set of verbs. Each verb has its own help too: `canvaslms
courses -h` shows the options of `courses` alone.

Exit the shell when you have read the help.
@

\section{Step 2: Check that you can reach Canvas}

This step is the reason the tutorial exists, and it is also the one
that must be worded with care.
[[canvaslms login]] asks for a hostname and an access token and then
\emph{overwrites} whatever credentials were stored before.
Canvas shows a new token only once, so a learner who already has a
working login and is told to run [[login]] again would destroy a setup
they cannot restore.
The step therefore asks for [[canvaslms courses]] first and makes
[[login]] conditional on the error message that [[courses]] prints when
there are no credentials.
The pattern only requires the [[courses]] command; whether [[login]] was
needed is the learner's situation, not something the tutorial can know.

<<reach canvas step>>=
# Check that you can reach Canvas

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)canvaslms\s+courses\b
hint: "The courses verb needs credentials; the login verb creates them. Run login only if courses complains about a missing hostname or token."
```

Run `canvaslms courses`.

If it prints your current courses, one per line, you are logged in
already. **Do not** run `canvaslms login` in that case: it replaces the
stored token, and Canvas shows a token only once.

If it instead says that there is no hostname or token, run
`canvaslms login` in this same shell. It asks for two things:

1. The hostname of your Canvas server, for example `canvas.kth.se`.
2. An access token. Open your profile settings in Canvas in a browser
   (`https://<hostname>/profile/settings`), create a new access token
   under **Approved integrations**, and paste it at the prompt.

Then run `canvaslms courses` again and check that your courses appear.
Exit the shell when they do.
@

\section{Step 3: Filter the list with a regex}

Steps 2 and 3 form a contrast: the command is the same, and only the
presence of a positional regex varies.
The learner sees the full list shrink to the courses whose code or name
matches, which is the first critical aspect, filtering rows.
The regex is the learner's own, so the pattern only checks that a
positional argument was given: a token after [[courses]] that does not
start with a dash.

<<filter courses step>>=
# Filter the list with a regex

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)canvaslms\s+courses\s+(?!-)\S
hint: "Add one positional argument after the verb: a regex that matches part of a course code or name. Quote it if it contains spaces."
```

Pick part of a course code or name from the list you just saw and run
`canvaslms courses` with it as an argument, for example
`canvaslms courses "DD1301"` or `canvaslms courses "programming"`.

The argument is a regular expression, matched case-insensitively
against the course code and the course name. Only the matching rows are
printed; the columns are the same as before. Try a second regex if the
first one matched nothing.

Exit the shell when you have a filtered list.
@

\section{Step 4: Ask for the Canvas identifier}

Now the regex is dropped and an option is added, so the learner sees the
other dimension: the same rows, but a different set of columns.
[[-i]] prepends the numeric Canvas identifier, which is what other tools
and the Canvas web address use to name a course.
Keeping the regex out of this step is deliberate; a step that varied
both the filter and the columns at once would not let the learner tell
which of them changed what.

<<add the id column step>>=
# Ask for the Canvas identifier

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)canvaslms\s+courses[^\n]*\s-i\b
hint: "One short option adds a column instead of removing rows. The help of the courses verb names it."
```

Run `canvaslms courses -i`.

Compare the output with step 2: the same courses are listed, but each
row now starts with a number. That is the Canvas identifier of the
course, the number you see in the course's web address. Options such as
`-i` change *what is printed about* each course; the regex in step 3
changed *which* courses are printed. The two can be combined.

Exit the shell when you have seen the identifiers.
@

\section{Step 5: Send the output to another program}

The columns are separated by tab characters precisely so that the
output can be handed to other programs, and this is the step where the
learner experiences that instead of being told.
[[cut -f1,3]] keeps the identifier and the course code; the pattern
accepts any pipe into [[cut]] so a learner who picks other columns still
passes.
Contrasting the piped command with step~4 keeps everything invariant
except the destination of the output.

<<pipe the output step>>=
# Send the output to another program

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)canvaslms\s+courses[^\n]*\|\s*cut\b
hint: "Pipe the previous command into cut and pick fields with -f. The fields are separated by tabs, which is cut's default."
```

Run `canvaslms courses -i | cut -f1,3`.

The columns are separated by tab characters, which is the default field
separator of `cut`, so `-f1,3` keeps the identifier and the course code
and drops the rest. Nothing about `canvaslms` changed between this step
and the previous one; only where its output goes. This is how
`canvaslms` is meant to be used in scripts: one command produces rows,
and standard tools such as `cut`, `grep`, `sort` and `wc` do the rest.

Exit the shell when you have the two-column output.
@

\section{Step 6: List the people in one course}

With the shape established on [[courses]], the tutorial generalises it
to a second verb.
[[users]] adds the one option that nearly every later command shares,
[[-c]], which selects the course by the same kind of regex the learner
used in step~3.
[[-s]] restricts the listing to students; without it, [[users]] prints
nobody, since it lists only the roles that were asked for.
The pattern requires both options so that a learner who forgets [[-s]]
and sees an empty listing is sent back to the instructions rather than
passed on an empty transcript.

<<list users step>>=
# List the people in one course

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)canvaslms\s+users[^\n]*\s-c\b
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)canvaslms\s+users[^\n]*\s-s\b
hint: "Two options: -c with a regex that selects one course, and -s to include the students."
```

Run `canvaslms users -c "<regex>" -s`, where `<regex>` is the course
regex you used in step 3.

The `-c` option selects the course, and it takes the same kind of regex
as the positional argument of `courses` did. Almost every `canvaslms`
verb that works inside a course has this option. The `-s` option asks
for students; `users` lists only the roles you ask for, so without `-s`
(or `-a` for teaching assistants) the output is empty.

Each row is the course, the user's login name and their full name,
separated by tabs, just like the course rows in step 2.

Exit the shell when you see the students.
@

\section{Step 7: Count them}

The last shell step fuses the two aspects the learner has now met
separately: the course selection from step~6 and the pipe from step~5.
Counting is the smallest useful thing to do with a list of students,
and it produces a single number, so the learner can check the result
against what Canvas shows in the browser.

<<count users step>>=
# Count them

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)canvaslms\s+users[^\n]*\|\s*wc\b
hint: "Pipe the previous command into wc -l. One row per student means one line per student."
```

Run the same `canvaslms users` command as in step 6, but pipe it into
`wc -l`.

Because `users` prints one row per student, `wc -l` prints the number
of students in the course. Compare it with the number Canvas shows on
the course's **People** page.

Exit the shell when you have the count.
@

\section{Step 8: Which flag picks the course?}

The tutorial closes by asking the learner to name the invariant they
have been using since step~6.
A single-select question makes the [[-c]] option explicit as the thing
to remember, in contrast to the three other short options they have
met, which each belonged to one verb.

<<name the course option step>>=
# Which flag picks the course?

```tutorial-step
kind: single_select
options:
  - -c
  - -u
  - -a
  - -i
answers:
  - -c
hint: "Look back at step 6: which option did users take that courses did not need?"
```

Which option selects the course in `canvaslms users`, and in most other
verbs that work inside a course?

You have now seen the shape of every `canvaslms` command: a verb, a
regex or option to narrow the result, and tab-separated output that
other programs can read. The tutorials `assignments-and-grading` and
`course-content` apply that shape to the rest of a course.
@
